  "doc.title": "How a CPU Runs a Program · a long essay you can play with",
  "doc.desc": "How does a line of code become clock-by-clock work inside a CPU, and why can the same program run several times faster with different code or a different CPU? Starting from the instruction set as a contract: RISC-V registers and its six instruction formats, hand-compiling a loop to machine code, the single-cycle datapath and its longest path, the five-stage pipeline, data hazards and forwarding, the cost of branches, the iron law of performance, how x86 splits instructions into micro-ops, and the 2026 frontier (RVA23, Armv9.8, APX). Every model is real: an assembler, an instruction interpreter and a cycle-by-cycle pipeline simulator.",
