  "hero.kicker": "Computing · Systems · a long essay you can play with",
  "hero.title": "How a CPU Runs a Program",
  "hero.sub": "What becomes of the one line of code you write? How does it turn into a cycle-by-cycle sequence of actions inside the CPU?<br>Why can the same program run several times faster when it is written another way, or on another CPU?",
  "hero.lead": "Follow one line of C, <code>sum += a[i];</code>, all the way down. First it becomes a few instructions defined by the instruction set, each one {xlen} bits of 0s and 1s. Then watch the simplest possible CPU finish one instruction in one clock cycle, and see why that is slow. Next, cut the work into five stages so that five instructions are in flight at once, and see how they get in each other's way and how the CPU works around it. Finally, use one formula to put the way the code is written and the chip it runs on into a single calculation. <strong>The models on this page are real</strong>: an assembler that encodes RISC-V as the specification describes, an interpreter that executes instructions one at a time, and a five-stage pipeline simulator that advances one cycle at a time.",
  "hero.note": "An AI wrote this page. The instruction encodings come from RISC-V International's official unprivileged ISA manual, and were checked one by one against an independent disassembler; the x86 bytes were produced by actually assembling the code. The datapath and pipeline are the classic teaching models from Patterson and Hennessy's textbook. <strong>Real CPUs are far more complicated</strong>, and each chapter ends by saying what its model leaves out. The component delays are textbook example numbers, not measurements of any chip. This page explains mechanisms only. It does <strong>not judge which company's chips or instruction sets are better</strong>.",

  "toc.ch1": "A contract: the instruction set",
