  "ch3.list.addr": "Address",
  "ch3.list.hex": "Machine code",
  "ch3.list.asm": "Assembly",
  "ch3.list.cnt": "Count",
  "ch3.regs.aria": "Current register values",
  "ch3.read.done": "Instructions executed",
  "ch3.read.total": "Total for this n",
  "ch3.read.per": "Instructions per element",
  "ch3.read.sum": "a0 (the sum)",
  "ch3.say.start": "Ready: the program counter points to address 0. The array holds 3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6.",
  "ch3.say.alu": "Executing <em>{asm}</em>: {rd} becomes {val}.",
  "ch3.say.load": "Executing <em>{asm}</em>: reads {val} from address {addr} into {rd}.",
  "ch3.say.store": "Executing <em>{asm}</em>: writes {val} to address {addr}.",
  "ch3.say.brT": "Executing <em>{asm}</em>: the condition holds, so it jumps back to address {to} for another pass.",
  "ch3.say.brN": "Executing <em>{asm}</em>: the condition fails, so it does not jump and the loop ends.",
  "ch3.say.ret": "Executing <em>{asm}</em>: returns to the caller. In all, {total} instructions were executed, and the sum is {sum}.",
  "ch3.p3": "Count the loop body: version 1 has {loop1} instructions per element, version 2 has {loop2}, and version 3 has {loop3}. With n = {nIron}, the three versions execute {ic1}, {ic2} and {ic3} instructions respectively. The CPU has not changed, and the sum is the same, but <strong>the work differs by a factor of two</strong>. This is the first half of the answer: changing the way the code is written changes the number of instructions.",
  "ch3.p4": "Note the <code>bne a1, t1, loop</code> in version 2: its offset is −12, so in machine code it is the scrambled B-type bit pattern from the previous chapter. The label <code>loop</code> exists only in the assembly text; the assembler replaces it with “12 bytes back”. The CPU sees only these {xlen}-bit numbers. That is not fundamentally different from the bytes the small machine in chapter 9 of “How a Pile of Switches Learned to Do Arithmetic” saw. They are just wider and more regular.",
  "ch3.limit": "The interpreter tracks only what the registers and memory hold after each instruction, which is the part the contract defines. It does not say how many cycles each one takes. From the next chapter on, the same program runs on real hardware structures.",

  "ch4.title": "Single cycle: one instruction per clock",
  "ch4.law": "The simplest CPU completes each instruction within one clock cycle: fetch the instruction, read the registers, compute, read or write memory, and write back. A set of control signals decides which paths are switched on during that cycle.",
  "ch4.p1": "Imagine a kitchen with one cook, who finishes each dish before starting the next: read the order, fetch the ingredients, chop, fry, plate. The small machine in chapter 9 of “How a Pile of Switches Learned to Do Arithmetic” worked like that kitchen: it finished one instruction before fetching the next. The <strong>single-cycle</strong> design goes further: every unit is laid out in a row, and an electrical signal starting at the program counter (PC) flows through the whole path in one cycle. When the clock ticks, the results are written at once to the registers, to memory and to the new PC.",
  "ch4.p2": "The figure shows a single-cycle RISC-V that can run our program. The PC supplies an address, and the <strong>instruction memory</strong> outputs a {xlen}-bit instruction. The rs1 and rs2 fields go straight to the <strong>register file</strong>, which returns two values (the alignment from the previous chapter pays off here). The <strong>immediate generator</strong> reassembles the constant from the bits scattered through the instruction. The <strong>ALU</strong> performs additions, subtractions and comparisons. The <strong>data memory</strong> is used when data is read or written. Finally, a multiplexer selects whether the value written back to a register is the ALU result or the value read from memory. Separately, one adder computes PC + 4, and another computes the branch target, PC + immediate; the branch result then selects which of them becomes the next PC.",
  "ch4.p3": "The <strong>control unit</strong> lets the same hardware execute different instructions. It looks only at the opcode and drives seven control lines: RegWrite (whether to write a register), ALUSrc (whether the ALU's second operand comes from a register or from the immediate), ALUOp (whether the ALU adds, compares, or looks further at funct3 and funct7), MemRead and MemWrite (whether to read or write data memory), MemtoReg (whether the value written back comes from memory or from the ALU), and Branch (whether this is a conditional branch).",
  "ch4.model.title": "Run the program through the single-cycle datapath, one signal at a time",
  "ch4.model.how": "This is the version 1 program from chapter 3, with n = 2. Press “Next” to step one instruction (one clock cycle) and see which units are in use, the values of the seven control lines, and the numbers entering and leaving the ALU. X means the value of that line does not matter.",
  "ch4.btn.next": "Next",
  "ch4.btn.prev": "Previous",
  "ch4.btn.reset": "Reset",
