  "ch4.op.2": "Look at funct3/7",
  "ch4.op.3": "Look at funct3",
  "ch4.read.ins": "Instruction this cycle",
  "ch4.read.pc": "PC",
  "ch4.read.alu": "ALU: inputs → output",
  "ch4.read.next": "Next PC",
  "ch4.say.R": "<em>{asm}</em> is R-type: ALUSrc = 0, so both operands come from registers. The ALU operation is chosen by funct3 and funct7. RegWrite = 1 and MemtoReg = 0, so the result {val} is written back to {rd}. Data memory is idle.",
  "ch4.say.I": "<em>{asm}</em> is an I-type operation: ALUSrc = 1, so the second operand is the immediate {imm}. The result {val} is written back to {rd}. Data memory is idle.",
  "ch4.say.load": "<em>{asm}</em> is a load: the ALU computes the address {addr}, MemRead = 1 reads {val}, and MemtoReg = 1 sends it into register {rd}. <strong>All five units are used</strong>. This is the longest path.",
  "ch4.say.store": "<em>{asm}</em> is a store: the ALU computes the address {addr}, and MemWrite = 1 writes {val} there. RegWrite = 0, so no register is written and MemtoReg is X.",
  "ch4.say.brT": "<em>{asm}</em> is a branch: the ALU compares the two values and the condition holds. Branch = 1, together with this result, makes the next PC select the branch target {to} instead of PC + 4.",
  "ch4.say.brN": "<em>{asm}</em> is a branch: the condition fails, so the next PC is still PC + 4 = {to}. Data memory and write-back are both idle.",
  "ch4.say.ret": "<em>{asm}</em> is jalr: the ALU computes the jump target (ra + 0), and that becomes the next PC. There is no jalr row in the control table; I added it by the same reasoning.",
  "ch4.p4": "Stepping through a few instructions shows that <strong>most instructions use only some of the units</strong>: addition does not use data memory, a branch does not use write-back, and a store does not use write-back either. Only lw uses all of them. But the clock does not know what the next instruction will be, so every cycle must be long enough for the longest path. The next chapter works out the cost.",
  "ch4.limit": "The R, lw, sw and branch rows of the control table come from chapter 4 of Patterson and Hennessy's RISC-V edition. The I-type operation and jalr rows are my additions, made by the same rules, and the ALUOp numbering is this page's own. Single-cycle designs are common in teaching and almost absent from products, for the reason in the next chapter.",

  "ch5.title": "The slowest instruction sets the clock",
  "ch5.law": "The clock period of a single-cycle CPU must cover the full path of the slowest instruction. Fast instructions wait just as long, so part of almost every cycle is wasted.",
  "ch5.p1": "A bus stops for five minutes at every stop, because the slowest passenger needs five minutes to board, though most passengers get on in half a minute. <a href='../switches-that-compute/#ch7'>Chapter 7 of “How a Pile of Switches Learned to Do Arithmetic”</a> explained that a cycle must cover the longest path between two stores, or the result is latched before it is ready. In a single-cycle CPU, “between two stores” means a whole instruction.",
  "ch5.p2": "Using the textbook example delays: a memory read takes {latMem} ps, a register-file read or write takes {latReg} ps, and an ALU operation takes {latAlu} ps. These are round teaching numbers, not measurements of any chip. Adding up the units each class of instruction passes through: a load takes {tLoad} ps, a store {tStore}, arithmetic {tAlu} and a branch {tBr}. The clock must be set to the longest of these: {period} ps.",
  "ch5.model.title": "How long each class of instruction takes, and how long the clock must be",
  "ch5.model.how": "Drag the three sliders to change the memory, ALU and register-file delays. Each row shows the units one class of instruction passes through. The gold vertical line is the single-cycle clock. The cyan vertical line is the clock for each stage when the path is cut into five stages (next chapter).",
  "ch5.model.aria": "Bar chart of delays for four classes of instruction, marking the single-cycle clock and the clock of one pipeline stage",
  "ch5.ctl.mem": "Memory delay",
  "ch5.ctl.alu": "ALU delay",
  "ch5.ctl.reg": "Register-file delay",
